  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "Negate the following statement: \n\n$\\,$ At least one prime number is not divisible by $5.$",
  "worked_solution": "Let $P$ be the set of all prime numbers. The given statement can be re-phrased as follows:\n\n$\\,$ There exists $p\\in P$ such that $5\u2224p.$\n\nIntroducing the existential quantifier, we get the following:\n\n$\\,$ $\u2203p\\in P,\\,5\u2224p.$\n\nNegating this, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u00ac(\u2203p\\in P,\\,5\u2224p) & \u2261\u2200p\\in P,\\,\u00ac(5\u2224p) \\\\  & \u2261\u2200p\\in P,\\,5\u2223p\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich reads \n\n$\\,$ For all $p\\in P,$ $5\u2223p.$\n\nor simply\n\n$\\,$ Every prime number is divisible by $5.$",
